import pathlib
p=pathlib.Path("src/commandlist.rs")
s=p.read_text()
header='''//! Stored command lists.
//!
//! Two kinds of lists are kept on disk: the command history and the
//! bookmarks. Both use exactly the same representation, which is what
//! this module implements.
//!
//! File format
//! -----------
//!
//! A list file contains its entries in order, oldest first. Entries
//! are separated by a line consisting of three dashes:
//!
//! ```text
//! echo one
//! ---
//! echo two
//! ---
//! a multi-line
//! command
//! ```
//!
//! Because the separator is a whole line, an entry may itself span
//! several lines. When a file is read back, the lines belonging to an
//! entry are joined with newlines again. Blank lines at the start or
//! end of an entry are not significant and are discarded, and empty
//! entries (for example between two consecutive separators) are
//! skipped entirely. Consecutive identical entries are also collapsed
//! into one; the same command may still appear several times in a
//! list as long as other entries are in between.
//!
//! Reading a file that does not exist, or that cannot be read as
//! UTF-8 text, simply yields an empty list. This keeps the program
//! usable when a list file is removed while it is not running, and
//! when a list is used for the first time.
//!
//! When a non-empty list has been read, it is written out again in
//! normalized form. That removes the artifacts described above (and
//! enforces the size limit for the history) even if the user never
//! changes the list during a session. If the list is empty, the file
//! is left untouched.
//!
//! Size limits
//! -----------
//!
//! The history is capped at a configurable number of entries. When it
//! grows past the limit, the oldest entries are dropped. Bookmarks
//! have no such cap.
//!
//! Saving
//! ------
//!
//! Saving a list writes the complete file at once, joining entries
//! with the separator line. There is deliberately no trailing newline
//! after the final entry. Writing is expected to succeed; a failure
//! (for example because the path is a directory) is treated as fatal,
//! because silently losing the history or the bookmarks would be more
//! surprising than reporting the problem.
//!
//! Editing a displayed list does not immediately change the stored
//! list. The displayed list is a working copy: confirming it (or
//! closing it with Escape) replaces the stored list with the working
//! copy and saves that, while discarding it by switching to another
//! view throws the working copy away and reloads from the stored
//! list the next time it is shown.
//!
//! The selection in a displayed list starts on its newest entry.
//! Moving up and down is clamped to the list, PageUp and PageDown jump
//! to the first and last entries, and deleting an entry removes it
//! from the working copy and keeps the selection on the entry that
//! slides into its place (or on the new last entry).
//!
'''
# remove one line if needed later
p.write_text(header+s)
